---
import PageLayout from '../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import ContentNote from '../components/content/ContentNote.astro';
import EvidenceTable from '../components/content/EvidenceTable.astro';
import RelatedReading from '../components/content/RelatedReading.astro';
---

<PageLayout
  title="Source Policy | Cooked Finance"
  description="How Cooked Finance selects primary sources, verifies data and definitions, labels model choices, and avoids unsupported financial claims."
  path="/source-policy"
  eyebrow="Evidence standards"
  heading="How we choose and use sources."
  lead="A citation is useful only when it actually supports the sentence beside it."
  updated="August 17, 2026"
>
  <h2>Source hierarchy</h2>
  <EvidenceTable caption="Our preferred evidence hierarchy">
    <thead>
      <tr><th scope="col">Priority</th><th scope="col">Source type</th><th scope="col">Typical use</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">1</th><td>Laws, regulations, regulators, official statistics, and public datasets</td><td>Definitions, consumer rights, policy, and population estimates</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">2</th><td>Original peer-reviewed research and documented institutional methodology</td><td>Research findings and planning assumptions</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">3</th><td>Official product, plan, or issuer documentation</td><td>Fees, eligibility, terms, and product behavior</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">4</th><td>Reputable secondary analysis with transparent sourcing</td><td>Context when a primary source is unavailable or difficult to interpret</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </EvidenceTable>
  <p>
    Search results, generated summaries, social posts, unattributed charts, and
    other websites repeating a number are discovery aids—not evidence by themselves.
  </p>

  <h2>How a claim is checked</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Open the original source rather than citing a search-result excerpt.</li>
    <li>Confirm that the source population, geography, date, unit, and definition match the claim.</li>
    <li>Check table notes, footnotes, denominators, inflation basis, sampling limitations, and revisions.</li>
    <li>Link as close as practical to the supporting page or table.</li>
    <li>Paraphrase accurately and keep direct quotation brief.</li>
    <li>State when Cooked Finance has converted units, rounded values, interpolated, or made an inference.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Statistics and benchmarks</h2>
  <p>
    A population benchmark must identify who was measured and what the measure
    includes. “Average net worth by age” is incomplete unless readers can see,
    for example, that the Federal Reserve reports both medians and means for SCF
    families grouped by the age of a reference person and values assets and
    liabilities under documented definitions.
  </p>
  <p>
    We do not derive exact age percentiles from a published median, infer individual
    results from family estimates without a warning, or present an interpolated
    value as though it were directly published. The
    <a href="/benchmarks/net-worth-by-age/">net-worth-by-age page</a> demonstrates
    the intended approach.
  </p>

  <h2>Planning guideposts</h2>
  <p>
    Institutional planning rules can be useful starting points, but they rely on
    assumptions. When the Cooked Score references Fidelity’s retirement savings
    milestones, the <a href="/methodology/">methodology</a> names the source,
    summarizes key assumptions, and labels Cooked Finance interpolation and
    scoring as proprietary choices. A guidepost is not presented as a guarantee
    or requirement for every reader.
  </p>

  <h2>Source recency</h2>
  <p>
    Newer is not automatically better; the correct choice is the newest reliable
    source that actually measures the claim. A triennial survey remains the
    appropriate source until a newer wave is released. Time-sensitive pages
    should display a meaningful review date and be revisited when their source
    changes.
  </p>

  <ContentNote title="Primary sources do not remove uncertainty" tone="caution">
    <p>
      Official data can still contain sampling error, imputation, revised
      definitions, reporting error, and time lag. We preserve relevant caveats
      rather than using an official logo as a substitute for interpretation.
    </p>
  </ContentNote>

  <h2>Conflicts and commercial sources</h2>
  <p>
    A company may be the best primary source for its own published assumptions,
    product terms, or fee schedule. That does not make its guidance neutral. We
    identify the company, link its assumptions, and avoid presenting marketing
    material as an independent standard. Payment does not determine which source
    supports an editorial claim.
  </p>

  <h2>Report a source problem</h2>
  <p>
    If a citation is broken, does not support the claim, or has been superseded,
    please send the page URL, disputed sentence, and better primary source through
    the <a href="/contact/">contact page</a>. Confirmed material issues are handled
    under the <a href="/corrections-policy/">corrections policy</a>.
  </p>

  <RelatedReading links={[
    { href: '/editorial-policy/', label: 'Editorial policy', description: 'How evidence becomes a reader-facing page.' },
    { href: '/corrections-policy/', label: 'Corrections policy', description: 'How source and interpretation errors are repaired.' },
    { href: '/methodology/', label: 'Model sources and formulas', description: 'See all external guideposts and proprietary choices together.' },
  ]} />
</PageLayout>
